Mitcad's patch to OCCT 8.0.1, under OCCT's licence:
SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-2.1-only WITH OCCT-exception-1.0

Fillets: the walked constant-radius blend that rolls on an edge (surface/
restriction) is a true rolling ball, and it turns about an end of that
edge (mitcad#237, with 0075).

Where a rounding overflows its face onto the edge beyond (the face is
narrower than the ball), OCCT walks a blend between the other face's
surface and that edge (BRepBlend_SurfRstConstRad). Its sections put both
contact points in the guide's normal plane and the centre along the
surface's normal projected into it; that is the rolling ball only where
the normal is square to the guide and the edge parallel to it. Elsewhere
the "ball" is not at the radius from the surface and the edge (a rib whose
far side tapers, radius 3: up to 6.5e-4 off; a pocket whose floor is a
lens narrower than the radius: 0.29 off), and the blend does not meet the
rolling-ball blend between two surfaces (0075) where the ball leaves the
edge for the face beside it. Where the edge ends at a vertex and the next
edge turns away from the ball, the ball must first turn about that vertex
(its contact held there) before it can roll on; OCCT has no such blend.

- BRepBlend_SurfRstConstRad (rolling by default; SetRollingBall(false)
  gives OCCT's sections): the equations are E(1) the mid-point of the
  contact points in the guide's normal plane (as 0075, so both blends meet
  at the same section), E(2) the edge tangent to the ball, (C - P).T = 0,
  and E(3) |C - P| = radius, with C = pts - radius n the centre along the
  surface's unit normal; derivatives of the unit normal as in 0075. The
  sections (Section, the variants for the approximation) are the ball's
  arcs in the plane through the centre holding both contact directions.
  Decroch (the ball leaves the edge for the reference face beside it):
  with the face's side of the edge given (SetRefSide), where the ball
  reaches into that face deeper than the confusion tolerance; otherwise as
  OCCT's, with the ball's plane. Pin(W) holds the contact at the edge's
  parameter W (E(2) becomes w = W): the ball pivots on that point; Centre
  gives the ball's centre; SetNextEdges gives the edges the ball rolls on
  next beyond the edge's ends. SetAxis: where the surface is one of
  revolution and the edge a circle on its axis, E(2) becomes "P in the
  plane through the axis and pts", which also holds where the centre lies
  on the axis (the ball touching both along circles, where E(2) is
  degenerate).
- BRepBlend_SurfCurvConstRadInv (the contact on the surface reaching an
  arc) and BRepBlend_SurfPointConstRadInv (the edge's contact at a vertex,
  with the edge's tangent given by SetTangent) solve the same equations.
  Blend_SurfRstFunction, Blend_SurfCurvFuncInv and Blend_SurfPointFuncInv
  get virtual Pin/Unpin, Centre, IsRollingBall, NextEdgeAtEnd and
  SetTangent whose defaults keep the other functions as they are.
- BRepBlend_SurfRstLineBuilder: at a start (or a first section) at an end
  of the edge, where the ball's centre lies behind the point (the edge
  runs away from it), or where the free ball a little further on does not
  roll on the edge and the next edge at that vertex runs away from it, the
  walk holds the contact there (StartPivot) and the ball turns about the
  point; the turn ends where the ball is tangent to the edge (or the next
  edge) there, found with the point inverse function, and the blend ends
  on the restriction (or at the vertex). While it turns, the contact on
  the edge is inside and does not leave it, and its transition is the
  direction it rolls into the edge from there. A contact on the edge that
  stays at a point while the surface's contact moves (the ball turning) is
  not a stop. A start whose solution lies outside the faces is taken a
  little further along the guide (the ball's contacts lead or trail the
  guide's plane). Where the walk on the edge can go no further (the
  solutions turn back where the ball comes to touch the reference face),
  it ends there by the inverse function to the surface/surface blend.
- ChFi3d_FilBuilder: the four constant-radius surface/restriction blends
  give the function the reference face's side of the edge (ChFi3d_RefSide,
  by classifying points beside the edge's middle), the next edges at its
  vertices (ChFi3d_NextEdges) and the axis of a coaxial circle
  (ChFi3d_CoaxialRestriction), and use the rolling inverse functions.

Upstream: the change is local to the surface/restriction functions and
their walk; the old sections stay behind SetRollingBall(false).
